**1. A powder-bed fusion method comprising:**  

depositing a layer of particulate material on a build surface, the layer including a first region and a second region spatially separate from the first region;  

selectively irradiating portions of the layer with a first laser to fuse particulate material according to a cross-section of an object being manufactured;  

irradiating the first region with a second laser to produce a first temperature profile in the first region; and  

irradiating the second region with a third laser to produce a second temperature profile in the second region, the second temperature profile differing from the first temperature profile in at least one of peak temperature, heating rate, cooling rate, temperature gradient, or duration above a threshold temperature,  

wherein the second and third lasers irradiate their respective regions during processing of the same layer, and wherein the second and third lasers are controlled independently of the first laser.

1. **A method of manufacturing a three-dimensional object, the method comprising:**  

   depositing, over a build plate, a particle layer comprising particles of a build material;  

   directing a first laser beam having a first beam characteristic onto a first region of the particle layer to thermally condition the particles in the first region without fully consolidating the particles;  

   directing a second laser beam having a second beam characteristic onto at least a portion of the thermally conditioned first region to consolidate the particles in accordance with a cross-section of the three-dimensional object;  

   directing a third laser beam having a third beam characteristic onto at least a portion of the consolidated particles to modify a material property of the consolidated particles, wherein the first, second, and third beam characteristics are distinct from one another and each comprise at least one of wavelength, power, energy density, spot size, intensity profile, or pulse duration; and  

   repeating the depositing and directing steps for successive particle layers to form the three-dimensional object on the build plate.

A method of additively manufacturing a three-dimensional article, the method comprising:

(a) depositing a layer of powder material over a build surface;

(b) selectively exposing a first region of the deposited layer to radiation generated by a first laser according to a first set of irradiation parameters, thereby producing a first thermal profile within the first region;

(c) selectively exposing a second region of the deposited layer to radiation generated by a second laser, distinct from the first laser, according to a second set of irradiation parameters, thereby producing a second thermal profile within the second region, the second thermal profile differing from the first thermal profile;

(d) fusing powder material within the first and second regions to form respective portions of the three-dimensional article; and

(e) repeating steps (a) through (d) for a plurality of successively deposited layers.

The first and second sets of irradiation parameters may differ with respect to one or more of laser power, beam diameter, scanning velocity, scan spacing, exposure duration, pulse frequency, scan pattern, or temporal sequencing, such that the first and second regions exhibit different heating rates, peak temperatures, dwell times, cooling rates, or combinations thereof.

1. **A process for additively manufacturing a three-dimensional article, the process comprising:**  

   positioning a layer of powder at a build location;  

   selectively irradiating portions of the layer with a first laser beam in accordance with layer data to fuse powder within the portions and form a fused cross-section of the article;  

   directing a second laser beam to a first region of the layer and controlling at least one irradiation parameter of the second laser beam to produce a first thermal condition in the first region;  

   directing a third laser beam to a second region of the layer, at least part of the second region being different from the first region, and controlling at least one irradiation parameter of the third laser beam to produce a second thermal condition in the second region; and  

   repeating the positioning, selectively irradiating, and directing steps for successive layers to form the article,  

   wherein the second and third laser beams are controlled independently of the first laser beam and of each other to provide region-specific control of a thermal history of the article before, during, or after fusion by the first laser beam.
